=F0=9F=93=84 10 Feb, </strong><em><strong>Exploring the Limit of Outcome Re=
ward for Learning Mathematical Reasoning</strong></em><strong>, https://arx=
iv.org/abs/2502.06781</strong></p><p>This paper explores how far RL with ju=
st binary "correct" or "wrong" feedback (like in DeepSeek-R1) can go for so=
lving math problems. To do this, they start by using Best-of-N sampling to =
collect positive examples and apply behavior cloning on them, which they sh=
ow is theoretically enough to optimize the policy.</p><p>To deal with the c=
hallenge of sparse rewards (especially when long chains of thought include =
partially correct steps) they add a token-level reward model that learns to=
 assign importance weights to different parts of the reasoning. This helps =
the model focus on the most critical steps when learning and improves the o=
verall performance.</p><div class=3D"captioned-image-container"><figure><a =
